radio chatter Home Secretary Nick Thomas  Simmons joins us now from South Wales  thank you for being on the program today  we've just been listening to Grant  Shapps of course there's legitimate  questions about whether the government's  too slow locking down too slow ramping  up testing getting protective equipment  to the frontline but at the same time  this is the week where they've hit their  target to get a hundred thousand tests  we heard Grant Shapps they're laying out  this idea of a new app and a new contact  tracing system that could be a way out  of lockdown would you give any credit to  the progress that the government's made  recently well we've been asking for the  ramping of the testing for some weeks so  clearly out and please the testing has  been ramped up I think there's always  been a difference between the number of  tests actually carried out and the  overall statistics and indeed the number  of people test it is different figure  but of course I'm pleased that testing  has been ramped up but that in itself is  not a strategy firstly the testing has  to be increased further I mean the  original target we were talking about a  quarter of million air tests a day some  time ago but it has to be linked as you  indicated in your questions Sophie to  tracing as well it's that testing and  tracing that is going to be so important  now in terms of easing the measures of  the lockdown in the weeks and months  ahead okay well you're talking there  about the potential for the lockdown and  in a route out of the the situation  we're in a minute that's going to come  up for review next week do you think  that the measure should be eased  well we won't support as always what the  best public health advice actually says  and I do welcome the fact that last week  the prime minister has indicated that he  has listened to what we've been saying  about publishing an exit strategy and  that is going to be extremely important  it's going to be important both to be  open with the public who've made so many  sacrifices in recent weeks as to what  the future now might hold but also for  our frontline public services so that  they can plan for the various scenarios  that scenarios are going to come in the  next few weeks you see calling for the  government to publish a plan in some  ways that was the easy bit the difficult  bit is working out what should be in  that plan so my question to you is is  there not time for labor to stick its  neck out and actually say what it is  that you would like to see I mean this  is your chance to influence what could  be the biggest decision that the  government makes or this government will  ever make so what measures do you think  it should be looked at well firstly  we've been arguing about the reopening  of schools for some weeks because every  child having access to education is so  important we've also been arguing about  looking at the different sectors of our  economy which which could see  restrictions more likely to be lifted  than others and of course the testing  and tracing strategy that I've already  mentioned and I know that Kirsten will  be speaking to the prime minister early  this week the Welsh Government has sent  over its ideas on an exit strategy and a  formal document as well but what  wouldn't be appropriate Sophie I think  is to set up some sort of rival plan we  will work with the government we want to  do this by agreement we want to support  the exit strategy we went into lockdown  with the political consensus with that  shared objective of minimizing loss of  life I think it's important to try to  come out of lockdown with the consensus  - so just to be clear the first thing  you mentioned there were schools  what does labour think should happen  with schools well we're not asking the  government for the impossible we're not  asking for at the moment a precise date  what we are simply asking for is a road  map as to how we can get our education  system back up but do you think that  road very much should be I mean you can  see other countries doing things  differently Germany prioritizing older  children about to sit exams you can see  France prioritizing nurseries and  primary schools what do you feel the  road batter should be well as I say  start setting out specific details in  that no one can say that they want to  see a road map when it comes to schools  I mean the difficult thing is coming up  with ideas that's no well yes but it is  for the government to come up with the  ideas we will then of course discuss  them and hopefully find  the consents on what they aren't  incidentally I would also say that the  UK government shouldn't be pulling some  sort of rabbit out of a habit they like  don't do work the devolved  administration's that's what I mean work  for the devolved administration's as  well of course hearing why was that  speaking to you it will be a decision  for the Welsh Education Minister but I  would like to see that by consensus we  want to see about the use of technology  we want to see whether it is as you say  Sophie particular age groups that might  come first particular parts of our  education system these are the kind of  things we would want the government to  look at but I'd only it would be helpful  just to simply set of a a rival plan  after having that argument let's move  forward by consensus let's see the  government proposals let's discuss them  next week that see the expert advice and  let's try and move forward by agreement  now and contact tracing of course  there's going to be a big part of our  lockdown exit strategy and you mentioned  it bit earlier in the interview but of  course it requires a certain number of  people to download the app do you think  people should be compelled to download  the app if they can't should there be  fines if you don't for example I think  there has to be a strategy or how we can  ensure there is a very large take-up of  this app app I think it's obvious that  we should need to do that we're willing  to discuss different options for it  however I think there are other matters  we need to take into account here as  well  not everybody has a smartphone in the  first place to which you could download  the app so what is the government's  strategy in relation to that but  secondly as well there are also issues  around privacy and security give you an  example Sophie there are people for whom  location services on their mobile  devices are turned off the particular  safety reasons and keeping themselves  safe oh so we need a strategy and a  parliamentary oversight I would suggest  as well in terms of overall how this is  going to work - now as a shadow home  secretary I'm interested to get your  view on the policing of the lockdown  because it does feel sometimes there's  been a bit of a postcode lottery  policing even individual officers  interpreting things differently at the  Crown Prosecution Service says it's  going to review every charge brought  under the emergency coronavirus laws is  that a good idea and  if you had any concerns about the way  the lockdowns been policed I think the  Crown Prosecution Service are sensible  to do that because we are asking the  police to police something that was  completely new and probably was  unthinkable even four or five months ago  but in general terms I think the police  have done an excellent job and I'm not  saying that they haven't been particular  incidents to which you have alluded in  your question Sophie but we've also got  to be clear that we must back our  frontline police here they've been asked  to do this by us they we passed the  coronavirus act through parliament back  at the end of March with this sweeping  set of powers to the government we then  asked the police to go out and enforce  the lockdown I think by and large  they've done an excellent job and I  think we really need to be thanking and  backing our frontline officers for all  they've done in very very difficult  circumstances now and it's always hard  at interviews to talk about anything  other than coronavirus it feels and but  I am keen to touch on just a couple of  other points now we have you on the  program a new opposition and you Shadow  Cabinet team and I wanted to ask you  about immigration as shadow home  secretary do you feel that free movement  of people should continue after we leave  the European Union well I think what we  will be doing over the next few months  and years coming into setting out our  immigration policy at the next general  election is listening to the concerns of  people but whilst of course we'll set up  that detail in due course I can set out  some broad principles I think firstly  rights about living working studying in  other countries are very important  they're part of the internationalism  that's in Labour's DNA  I think fairness is also going to be so  so important we will never treat people  as statistics in our immigration system  we will always treat them as people and  of course justice is important as well  it's not some abstract principle we've  seen in the windrow scandal what happens  when we lose sight of that in our  immigration system so they are the broad  principles that we'll be drawing on as  we move forward to set out our position  in more detail in the years ahead  so those the broad principles but I am  interested in specifics as well I mean  que estar mo in the leadership contest  said that he thought labour should argue  in favor of free movement of people so  do you think three million people should  continue we will always make the  positive case or immigration and the  great benefits that immigration has  brought to our country but clearly it's  not quite what I asked but firstly we  will be elite would have left the  European we have left the European Union  and the transition people have ended by  the time we get to the next general  election it will be this government  there will have negotiated that new  agreement with the European Union we're  also going through the coronavirus  crisis where vast parts of the world are  in lockdown you can't even travel from  one part of the world to another so we  will of course be shaping our  immigration policy around those events  and those changes and by the way I would  also say that what we've seen in the  coronavirus crisis is the value of  people refuse collectors are shop  workers are nurses are local government  workers all of whom are doing a  wonderful job keeping our country  running where their salary doesn't  reflect their value and the government  is trying to convince us that anyone who  name less than twenty five thousand six  hundred fans a year is unskilled some of  us didn't need reminding that that  wasn't the case but if anyone did need  reminding I think we've seen in the  coronavirus crisis the value of people  who are on salaries that are lower than  that and that doesn't reflect what they  do and just finally as well the other  thing we've seen of course in the  coronavirus crisis is that those from  VME backgrounds appear to be more at  risk or serious complications now public  health England has given Trevor Phillips  a role in to the inquiry into why that  might be  now some campaigners have protested that  he shouldn't be a part of that inquiry  because the past comments he's made  about Muslims others say that this is a  man who spent his life campaigning for  racial inequality it's his friends in  the labour party who should be sticking  up for him kids Thomas says that the  government should engage with the  concerns of the campaigners is that the  right decision or should co-star might  be supporting Trevor Phillips Nokia star  is quite right  say that we should be engaging with  those concerns I think that's entirely  the right approach given the issues that  have been raised but I should also say  that we as a Labour Party have asked  Doreen Lorenz who's got obviously you  know it was great campaigning history in  this area after such a tragic event with  the murder of course of Stephen Lawrence  she will be looking at it for us and  this thing control issue going forward  looking at the impact of the corona  virus and it's disparate impact it seems  on baam unities which is something we do  need to learn the lessons of going  forward okay Nick Thomas Simmons thank  you very much for being on the program  and talking about variety things thank  you thank you

